# Family continuation theory

Umbrella for the route "a zero-free half-plane by continuing the reciprocals of a family of
`L`-functions across its rightmost zeros".

* **Continuation from a common signal.** A kernel `G` holomorphic on `Re s > 1` with decay on
  vertical strips, whose signal `(1/2π) ∫ G(2+it) Z^{2+it} dt` is `O(Z^b)`, extends to `Re s > b`;
  if `G L = E` with `E` zero-free, `L` has no zero in `Re s > b`. A sum with a direct bound and
  a comparison with the signal gives the half-plane right of the larger exponent. The frame holds
  for the `L`-functions of the nontrivial ray class characters of a number field, with the
  kernels `exp((s − c)²)/L(s, ψ)`; their signal is the smoothed Möbius sum
  `Σ_S (−1)^{|S|} ψ(∏ S) w(Z/N(∏ S))` over the finite sets of primes, with the log-normal weight
  `w(y) = (2√π)⁻¹ y^c exp(−(log y)²/4)`, and a bound `O(Z^b)` for it excludes the zeros of
  `L(s, ψ)` in `Re s > b`. The pole-cleared function `(s − 1) L(s, ψ)` vanishes at `1` for a
  nontrivial character, so the family steps stated with it have an unsatisfiable premise as soon
  as `K` has a nontrivial ray class character. The step that reads only the zeros of `L(s, ψ)`
  off `1`, with the kernels `exp((s − c)²) H(s)/L(s, ψ)` of the source, covers every ray class
  character and produces `HeckeHalfPlane K σ₀`. Over `ℚ` the trivial character of the unit
  modulus is `ζ`: `HeckeHalfPlane ℚ σ₀` puts the zeros of `ξ` in `Re ρ ≤ σ₀`. A comparison with
  an auxiliary height `Z^τ`, with losses `(1 + Z^τ)^A` and `Z^B (Z^τ)^{−N}` for every small `τ`
  and every `N`, keeps half of its saving, whatever the orders `A`, `B`; the family step holds
  with comparisons of this form. Among the largest real parts of the zeros of a row in `I`
  nested height windows, two consecutive ones differ by at most `e` when `(I − 1) e` exceeds the
  width of the strip; rounding to a grid gives the bin of the row with its buffer.
  The family step holds with signals `exp((s − c)²) H(s)/L(s, ψ)`, `H` holomorphic and
  nonvanishing right of the boundary and bounded on `Re s ≥ 2`: the local factors that an identity
  leaves over. The correction of the principal row of the cubic-theta probe is such an `H`: its
  local factor at the double residue is `(1 − q²)(1 − q(R − D)/(1 − D))/(1 − R)`, its defect is
  `O(N(P)^{−min(6σ−3, 1+σ, 2)})`, so the Euler product converges on `Re s > 2/3`, and after
  deleting the primes of small norm it lies within `1/2` of `1`. For the third cyclotomic field
  the two estimates for every ray class character, with this correction, give
  `zetaZeroAbscissa ≤ σ₀`: the half-plane route as one implication.
* **The signal of `ζ`.** The kernel `exp((s − c)²)/((s − 1)ζ(s))` is a signal kernel; a bound
  `O(Z^b)`, `b < 1`, for its signal gives the half-plane `Re s > b` and positivity of the dressed
  lag aggregate on large primes at every level above `b + ½`; bounds with every exponent above
  `½` give the Riemann hypothesis. The signal is the smoothed Möbius sum
  `Σ_n μ(n) · signal (poleKernel c) 2 (Z/n)`. Conversely a zero-free half-plane `Re s > Θ` bounds
  the signal by `O(Z^b)` for every `b > Θ`: the zero abscissa is at most `b` exactly when the
  signal is `O(Z^{b'})` for every `b' > b`.
* **Additive columns of residue characters.** The correlation of two columns of coprime moduli is
  one term; a weighted second moment over the rows is a multiplicative form in the frequency; at
  the frequencies on which the characters are trivial the terms add without cancellation. Poisson
  summation in the row of a column times a multiplicative row twist is one column value times the
  transform of the twist, for a character its Gauss sum. At a prime common to two columns with
  the same nonprincipal character the local factor of the correlation is `−χ(b/a)` at the
  frequencies prime to it and `(N − 1) χ(b/a)` at the others.
* **The sign of the source.** The `n`-th power of a Gauss sum of order `n > 2` is `−card F` for
  odd `n` and `χ(−1) · card F` for even `n`, modulo `(μ − 1)²` in `ℤ[μ]`: odd orders carry the
  Möbius sign, and three is the least of them. For two characters with quadratic product,
  `g(χ) g(φ) = J(χ, φ) g(χ φ)` with `J ≡ −1`: the row coefficient `χ` and the row twist `φ` of a
  probe are such a pair (cubic and sextic, order four twice, sextic and cubic).
* **The mean square of the additive polynomial.** The large sieve for points of the torus `ℝ²/ℤ²`
  separated in the maximum of the two circle distances; its form over the Eisenstein integers
  with the self-dual character `exp(2πi Tr(w/√−3))`; fractions `d/s` with `|s|² ≤ Y`, distinct
  modulo `ℤ[ω]`, are `1/Y`-separated, and the mean square over a row box of side `N` is at most
  `(2N + 2Y ∑_{k≤M} 1/k)² ∑ |c|²`; there are at most `(2 + 2Y ∑ 1/k)²` such fractions, which
  bounds the mean square for bounded coefficients.
* **The transfer to `ζ`.** The number of Eisenstein integers of norm `n` is `6 Σ_{d ∣ n} χ₃(d)`, and
  `Σ_{(m,n) ≠ 0} (m² − mn + n²)^{−s} = 6 ζ(s) L(s, χ₋₃)` for `Re s > 1`: the zeta function of
  `ℚ(√−3)` carries the zeros of `ζ`; with a Dirichlet character `χ` of the norm the sum is
  `6 L(s, χ) L(s, χ χ₋₃)`. For a number field `K` with `IsCyclotomicExtension {3} ℚ K` the ring
  of integers has the basis `1, η`, norm form `a² − ab + b²`, six units and `Σ_{d ∣ n} χ₋₃(d)`
  ideals of norm `n`; `ζ_K = ζ · L(·, χ₋₃)`, and `HeckeHalfPlane K σ₀` puts the zeros of `ξ` in
  `Re ρ ≤ σ₀`.
* **The positive form.** A row occurs again at every index `u aⁿ` of a family of twists by an
  `n`-th power residue symbol; a mean square `T` of the long family bounds the rows of
  multiplicity `m` by `T/m`. A generic mean square over the twists of norm up to `Z^θ` gives the
  half-plane `½ + (1 − 1/n) θ/2`: for the sextic symbol `11/12`, `7/8`, `13/15`, `5/6` at
  `θ = 1`, `9/10`, `22/25`, `4/5`.
* **The budget of the compensated probe.** Low boundary `5/6 + M/12 − ℓ/6 + (M + ℓ − 1)₊/2` in the
  row length `M` and slot length `ℓ`; the face of zero retained length is paid by the tuple
  factor; the high exponent of the top rows is at most `−49/440640` at the published geometry and
  `−7/30672` in the bin `δ = 79/204`; every geometry with low boundary at most `7/8 − 1/4000` has
  a positive high exponent in that bin. In the bin of rows without zeros right of the line, summed
  trivially, every geometry with low boundary below `13/15` has a positive high exponent: the
  room inside the probe is `[13/15, 7/8]`. Expressed through the Möbius sums of the twists, the
  mean square of the rows contains the target with multiplicity and full prime amplitude; with
  the excess `(2 + 2ℓ − M)/6 + 2β − 2` of these principal twists at a zero `β`, the low boundary
  is `β` identically, so a bound for the rows through the twists is no low estimate. Without the
  dual-length term the low boundary is at least `5/6`. Designs with more row variables, or with
  frequencies of lower order and rows of larger conductor, have larger boundaries in the same
  model.
* **The exponent plane of the low side.** In the range of the reflection the low boundary is
  `5/6 + (M − 2ℓ)/12`; with the allowance of the marked rows inactive it is at least `13/15`.
  The reflection of a row and the summation in the row index are two reflections of the plane of
  exponents, and their product is a translation in scale. In the positive form a term `K^u N^v`
  of the large sieve for the reflected rows with `4u + 3v ≥ 5` gives no half-plane: the square
  row indices (`K^{1/2} N`), one summation applied to the trivial bound (`N³/K`) and a bound by
  the square root of the conductor (`N²`) are on or past this threshold; the optimal sieve gives
  `11/12`, and a term `(KN)^{2/3}` would give `71/72`. Without the reflection the sextic large
  sieve gives no half-plane.
* **From a half-plane to the line.** The transfer from return-free intervals of the prime orbit to
  bounded prime traces follows from the Riemann hypothesis, and under a zero-free strip it is
  equivalent to it.
* **The shortest family with a generic mean square.** For smoothed Möbius sums of length `Z`
  twisted by residue symbols of order `n ≥ 2` up to norm `Z^θ`, the principal `n`-th-power
  subfamily has generic mean square `O_ε(Z^{θ + 1 + ε})` if and only if
  `θ ≥ (n/(n − 1))(2 Θ_ζ − 1)`, an attained minimum that vanishes if and only if the Riemann
  hypothesis holds. Replacing `μ(m)` by `μ(m)²` shifts the exact minimum unconditionally to
  `n/(n − 1)`: every `θ < n/(n − 1)` requires Möbius sign cancellation across `m`. Over all odd
  moduli `1 ≤ u ≤ Z^θ` weighted by the Jacobi symbol `(m / u)` (`n = 2`), generic mean square at
  `θ > 0` puts `Θ_ζ` and `zeroAbscissa χ_{u₀}` for every odd squarefree `u₀ > 1` at most at
  `min(1, ½ + θ/4)`, and by a conductor-uniform subpolynomial lower bound for primitive Dirichlet
  `L`-functions, generic mean square holds down to `θ → 0⁺` (and for all `θ ≥ 0`) if and only if
  GRH holds for `ζ` and all odd primitive real Dirichlet characters. Rewriting the reflection-and-sieve
  producer class in `θ = (2n/(n − 1))(σ₀ − ½)` gives sharp floors `10/9` (optimal sextic
  multiplicative large sieve), `1` (additive planar sieve / dual diagonal), `9/10` (published
  binding bin), `(5n − 8)/(5(n − 1))` (`22/25` at `n = 6`, trivial floor bin across all `n ≥ 2`
  and both Gram regimes), and `(n − 2)/(n − 1)` (`4/5` at `n = 6`, free reflection-sieve floor).
* **The floor-bin bilinear signed Gram identity.** Cauchy–Schwarz over the column shell `M` bounds
  `‖∑_{u ∈ B₀} α(u) ∑_{m ∈ M} b(m) Φ(u, m)‖²` by `(∑_m ‖b(m)‖²)` times the diagonal plus the real
  part of the signed off-diagonal row correlation `∑_{u₁ ≠ u₂} α(u₁) conj(α(u₂)) G_M(u₁, u₂)`,
  preserving relative phases across floor-bin rows without zeros right of `½`. Unimodular matched
  phases, character-group Parseval enlargement, and polynomial-approximant completion saturate at
  `R₀ = 1`. On the balanced edge at `q = δ/2`, zero-density excess `R = 1 − δ + η` interpolates
  `((5 + 6η)n − 8)/((5 + 6η)(n − 1))` across `[22/25, 9/10]`, while crossing `< 22/25` requires
  uniform signed cancellation `R ≤ (3 + ℓ)/(2(1 + 3ℓ)) − δ` (met by `R = (1 − δ)/2` down to
  `(n − 2)/(n − 1)` for `2 ≤ n ≤ 6`). Under floor-bin exponent `R₀ ∈ [½, 1]`, the minimum of
  `probeThetaGen` transitions at `R_crit(n) = ½ + 1/n` from `((6 R₀ − 1)n − 8)/((6 R₀ − 1)(n − 1))`
  to the `lx = 0` boundary curve `signedGramBrokenTheta n R₀`.
* **Metaplectic Voronoi exponent invariants.** Generalized row reflections `(r, l) ↦ (r, κ r − l)`
  and row-index summations `(r, l) ↦ (κ' l − r, l)` generate `SL₂(ℝ)` matrices of trace `κ κ' − 2`
  preserving `κ r² − κ κ' r l + κ' l²` (elliptic for `κ κ' < 4`, parabolic with unique linear
  invariant `(κ/2) r − l` at `κ κ' = 4`, hyperbolic without nonzero linear invariants and strictly
  larger dual excess for `κ > 2, κ' = 2`). Unitary Plancherel `L²`-invariance, hybrid slot excess
  `slotHybridExcess ≥ max(0, (2ℓ − M)/3)` (removing `2ℓ ≤ lx + ly` unconditionally across all
  `(lx, ly, ℓ, v) ∈ ℝ⁴` for `n ≥ 3`), `k`-variable scaling `k(n − 2)/(n − 1)`, and Weyl-orbit twist
  circularity lock every Cauchy–Schwarz `L²` Voronoi sieve at `θ ≥ (n − 2)/(n − 1)`, leaving
  pre-Cauchy–Schwarz bilinear row-column cancellation `sBilin > 0` (`θ ≥ (n − 2)/(n − 1) −
  (2n/(n − 1)) sBilin`) as the unique escape route below `(n − 2)/(n − 1)`.
* **Grossencharacter coupling and the three-fifths reach of the cubic probe frame.** Over
  `K = ℚ(ω)`, the sixth power of the local probe phase `(π̄_v / √N(v)) η(v)` at every prime `v`
  away from `λ = ω − 1` equals `ψ₆(v) η⁶(v)`, where `ψ₆((a)) = (ā / |a|)⁶` is the sextic
  archimedean Grossencharacter on ideals of `ℤ[ω]`. Factoring `(1 − ψ₆(v) η⁶(v) N(v)^{3 − 6s})⁻¹`
  out of `principalLocalFactor` leaves a residual Euler product holomorphic, bounded on `Re s ≥ 2`,
  and everywhere nonvanishing on `Re s > ½` without prime-norm cutoff. Two-dimensional harmonic
  Gaussian self-duality and Poisson summation on conductor cosets continue the primitive
  Grossencharacter `L`-functions `L(w, ψ₆^k ψ)` (`k ≥ 1`) to entire completed `L`-functions of
  order `1` with functional equation `Λ(1 − w) = ε(ψ, k) conj(Λ(conj w))`; `3 + 4 cos θ + cos 2θ ≥ 0`
  eliminates zeros on the closed half-plane `Re w ≥ 1`, while Hadamard subquadratic rigidity forces
  infinitely many zeros with unbounded ordinates in `½ ≤ Re w < 1`. Consequently, the uncoupled
  probe frame reaches `Re s ≥ 2/3` with fixed cutoff `N₀ = 160` and fails unconditionally below
  `2/3` (`7/12 ≤ Re s < 2/3` contains infinitely many zeros of `continuedProbeCorrection`), clearing
  `L^S(6s − 3, ψ₆ η⁶)` incurs an unconditional `c · Z^{2/3}` squarefree Möbius norm floor, and
  coupling the infinite-order Grossencharacter tower `{ψ₆^{k_r} η^{6^r}}` (`k₀ = 0`,
  `k_{r+1} = 6 k_r + 1`) contracts `b ↦ (3 + b)/6` from `1` to `max(σ₀, 3/5)` unconditionally
